          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Hepatic cirrhosis causes splenomegaly through which mechanistic category?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Hepatic cirrhosis causes splenomegaly through which mechanistic category?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Infective — macrophage expansion from organism engulfment</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Infective splenomegaly involves macrophage expansion and immune follicular hyperplasia in response to microbial antigens, not passive venous congestion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Congestive — raised splenic venous pressure</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cirrhosis creates an intrahepatic block, raising portal venous pressure and passively engorging the red pulp sinusoids — the hallmark of congestive splenomegaly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Infiltrative — metabolic substrate accumulation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Infiltrative splenomegaly involves accumulation of metabolic substrates (e.g. glucocerebroside in Gaucher disease) inside macrophages, not vascular congestion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haematological — malignant cell infiltration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haematological splenomegaly results from malignant haematopoietic cell infiltration (e.g. CML) or work hypertrophy from haemolysis, not venous backpressure from liver disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Cirrhosis raises portal venous pressure, causing passive sinusoidal congestion in the spleen — this is the congestive category, not infective, infiltrative, or haematological."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Cirrhosis drives portal hypertension, which passively engorges the splenic sinusoids — a congestive mechanism."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
