          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with massive splenomegaly and portal hypertension but near-no</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with massive splenomegaly and portal hypertension but near-normal liver function tests. Which cause of congestive splenomegaly best explains this combination?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatic cirrhosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In cirrhosis, the intrahepatic fibrotic block typically impairs hepatocyte function, producing deranged LFTs, coagulopathy, and hypoalbuminaemia alongside splenomegaly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Budd-Chiari syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein thrombosis) creates a post-hepatic block, usually presenting with painful hepatomegaly, ascites, and abnormal liver function.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis (idiopathic hepatoportal sclerosis) causes portal hypertension and massive splenomegaly while liver function remains largely preserved — a key distinguishing feature from cirrhosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic malaria</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic malaria causes infective splenomegaly through red pulp macrophage expansion and haemozoin deposition, not congestive splenomegaly from portal hypertension.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The combination of massive splenomegaly with preserved liver function points to non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis. Cirrhosis and Budd-Chiari impair liver function; malaria is infective, not congestive."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis is an important Indian cause of massive splenomegaly where portal hypertension exists but hepatocyte function is preserved."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
