          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Overwhelming Post-Splenectomy Infection (OPSI) is primarily caused by which grou</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Overwhelming Post-Splenectomy Infection (OPSI) is primarily caused by which group of organisms?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intracellular parasites such as Leishmania and Plasmodium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While the spleen helps clear parasitised red cells, OPSI specifically results from loss of IgM-mediated opsonisation of encapsulated bacteria, not intracellular parasites.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anaerobic organisms such as Clostridium and Bacteroides</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Anaerobic infections are not primarily cleared by splenic opsonisation. OPSI targets organisms that require the IgM and complement pathway the spleen provides.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, and Neisseria meningitidis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The spleen's white pulp generates IgM that opsonises encapsulated bacteria. Without it, these organisms cause fulminant sepsis with 50–70% mortality. Vaccination against all three before elective splenectomy is mandatory.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gram-negative enteric bacilli such as Escherichia coli and Klebsiella</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Enteric gram-negative organisms are cleared by hepatic Kupffer cells and neutrophils. OPSI specifically involves encapsulated bacteria that depend on splenic IgM for opsonisation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "OPSI results from encapsulated bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae type b, N. meningitidis) that require splenic IgM for opsonisation. Vaccination against all three before elective splenectomy is essential."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Loss of splenic IgM opsonisation makes encapsulated bacteria — pneumococcus, H. influenzae type b, and meningococcus — the primary threat after splenectomy."
                }
              ],
